(* Note that the analytic versions of these rules are very incomplete!!! EA *)
&
\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
%
% RULE REmptysubstitution1

                     (* substitution of seEMPTY in a "por"-phrase *)

< m1: I1::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ I2:mu2,
                      porobjrel/VARPREPP{VARPREPPrec1}
                            [ head/PREP(KEY_doorporprepkey){PREPrec1},
                              objrel/I3::EMPTYVAR{EMPTYVARrec1}
                            ],
                      mu4
                    ],
             mu3
           ]
SUBST:
  m2:EMPTY(KEY_seemptykey){EMPTYrec1}                (*substituent*) 
>

< m :I1::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ mu1,
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                     [ I2:mu2,
                       mu4
                     ],
              mu3
            ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: CLAUSErec1.voice IN [Passive,SEpassive] 
       I2: (NOT EXIST(mu2, [porobjrel/..]) )
       I3: EMPTYVARrec1.index = LEVEL
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
      >


      COMP
         <
          C1: (EMPTYVARrec1 = 
                     { persona : 3
                       numero  : singular
                       genero  : masc
                     })                          AND
              (VARPREPPrec1 = PREPPrecdef EXCEPT FOR 
                       [actsubcefs, synppefs, headkey ]) AND
              (VARPREPPrec1 = 
                       {
                         actsubcefs  : [doorpor]
                         synppefs    : [synEMPTY]
                         headkey     : KEY_doorporprepkey
                       } )
          A1: @
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: EMPTYVARrec1 := 
                     { index   : LEVEL
                       persona : 3
                       numero  : singular
                       genero  : masc
                     };
              VARPREPPrec1 := 
                       {
                         actsubcefs  : [doorpor]
                         synppefs    : [synEMPTY]
                         headkey     : KEY_doorporprepkey
                       }
              (* all other attributes retain their default values *)
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] REmptysubstitution1
\item[* relevant examples:]
x2 fue le\'{\i}do [por x1] $\rightarrow$ x2 fue le\'{\i}do\\
\item[* language:] Spanish
\item[* kind of rule:] M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] RC: EMPTYsubstitution
\item[* date of creation:] 29-FEB-1988 15:20:00  
\item[* author:] Elly van Munster
\item[* copied from:] Jan Odijk
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 18/01/89\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo\\
Added mu4.
\item date: 25/01/89\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo\\
changed numero and genero of EMPTYVAR in correspondence with seempty.
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To substitute seEMPTY as the obj of a porobjrel/..
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]dna
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:] Existential Quantification.
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{itemize}
\item We assume there are four different EMPTY's:
\begin{enumerate}
\item seEMPTY (Dutch "men")
\item ellosEMPTY (Dutch "ze generic")
\item emptyEMPTY (Dutch "empty argument")
\item unoEMPTY (Dutch "je generic")
\end{enumerate}
\item The following EMPTYsubst-rules have been made:
\begin{enumerate}
\item seEMPTY in a por-phrase (Active and SEactive).
\item emptyEMPTY as objrel (Active).
\item emptyEMPTY as indobjrel (Active).
\item seEMPTY as subjrel (SEactive).
\item ellosEMPTY as subjrel (SEactive).
\item seEMPTY as subjrel $\rightarrow$ {\bf UNO} with refl.V. (Dutch "men").
\item seEMPTY as subjrel $\rightarrow$ {\bf UNO} with non-refl.V. (Dutch "men").
This rule has a very low bonus but is necessary for cases where later on the 
derivation the occurrence of a second SE blocks rule 4.\\
(-*Se se lava las manos $\rightarrow$ {\em} uno se lava las manos).
\item unoEMPTY as subjrel (Dutch "je generic").
\item seEMPTY as indobjrel (Dutch vp120 with "men" as subj into Spanish 
vp012 or vp021
with EMPTYVAR in indobj-position, translated into "les..a la gente"; 
e.g.:\\
- Men houdt van kaas. $\rightarrow$ Les gusta el queso a la gente.\\
- Men zwemt graag. $\rightarrow$ Les gusta nadar a la gente
\item seEMPTY as objrel (SEactive, ergative verb, vp010).
\end{enumerate}
\item The following translations D-Sp are obtained:\\
- Men danst $\rightarrow$ \\ - Se baila \\
- Er wordt gedanst $\rightarrow$ \\ - Se baila \\
- Men schaamt zich $\rightarrow$ \\ - Uno se averg\"{u}enza \\
- Men wast z'n handen $\rightarrow$ \\ - Uno se lava las manos \\
- Ze dansen $\rightarrow$ \\ - Bailan \\
- Er worden appels gegeten $\rightarrow$ \\ - Se comen manzanas \\
- De kinderen (non-generic!) worden gezocht $\rightarrow$ \\ - Se busca a los 
  chicos \\
  - Los chicos son buscados \\
- Er worden kinderen (generic!) gezocht $\rightarrow$ \\ - Se buscan ni\~{n}os \\
- Het boek wordt door Jan gelezen $\rightarrow$ \\ - Juan lee el libro \\
  - El libro es le\'{\i}do por Juan 
\item I put the porobjrel/.. VERBP-final, in order to avoid two adjacent mu's, 
which would make the rule much more complicated.
\end{itemize}
\end{description}


\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% RULE REmptysubstitution2

                     (* subst of emptyEMPTY as an objrel/.. *)

< m1: I1::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
                [ mu1,
                  predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                         [ mu2,
                           I6:head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                           cliticrel/CLITIC{CLITICrec1}[mu4],
                           I5:mu7,
                           objrel/I4::EMPTYVAR{EMPTYVARrec1},
                           I3:mu5
                         ],
                  mu6
                ]
SUBST:
  m2: EMPTY(KEY_emptyemptykey){EMPTYrec1}            (*substituent*)
>

< m : I2::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec2}
                [ mu1,
                  predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                         [ mu2,
                           I6:head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                           cliticrel/CLITIC{CLITICrec1}[mu4],
                           I5:mu7,
                           I3:mu5
                         ],
                  mu6
                ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: CLAUSErec1.synvpefs * AUX_emptyobjvps <> []
       I2: CLAUSErec2.synvpefs * AUX_emptyobjvps <> []
       I3: NOT EXIST (mu3, [objrel/..])
       I4: EMPTYVARrec1.index = LEVEL
       I5: NOT EXIST (mu7, [objrel/..])
       I6: NOT VERBrec1.thetavp IN [vp010, vp012, vp021] (* niet ergatief *)
      >


   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
      >


      COMP
         <
          C1: EMPTYVARrec1 = 
                     { persona : 3
                       numero  : omeganumero
                       genero  : omegagenero
                     }      
          A1: CLAUSErec2 := CLAUSErec1;
              CLAUSErec2.synvpefs := CLAUSErec1.synvpefs * AUX_emptyobjvps
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: EMPTYVARrec1 :=
                     { index   : LEVEL
                       persona : 3
                       numero  : omeganumero
                       genero  : omegagenero
                     };
              CLAUSErec1 := CLAUSErec2;
              CLAUSErec1.synvpefs := CLAUSErec2.synvpefs * AUX_emptyobjvps
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] REmptysubstitution2
\item[* relevant examples:]

x1 come x2  $\rightarrow$ x1 come (Juan come) \\
$<$EMPTYVAR$>$ come x2 $\rightarrow$ $<$EMPTYVAR$>$ come (Se come)
\item[* language:] Spanish
\item[* kind of rule:] M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoClause
\item[* class:] RC: EMPTYsubstitution
\item[* date of creation:] 01-MAR-1988 11:00:00  
\item[* author:] Elly van Munster
\item[* copied from:] Jan Odijk
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 18/01/89\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo\\
Added mu7.
\item date: 13/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo\\
Added I6. This rule is for object empty of a non-ergative verb.
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To substitute emptyEMPTY as objrel/..
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] dna
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:] Existential Quantification.
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item I specified the head/VERB and cliticrel/CLITIC in the model in order to 
avoid to adjacent mu's in the outputmodel, which would make the rule much more 
complicated.
\item No mu is needed between cliticrel/CLITIC and objrel/EMPTYVAR, since in
the verbpattern-rules the objrel/EMPTYVAR is always immediately following 
the VERB.
\end{enumerate}
\end{description}


